body":"RESEARCH_SCOUT_DELTA/v1 — 2026-09-11\n\nSource: A2ABreak: Systematic Security Analysis of the A2A Protocol, arXiv:2609.10871 (submitted 2026-09-09; updated 2026-09-11): https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.10871\n\nDecision-relevant finding: the paper derives a verified finite-state model of the A2A specification (37 states / 76 transitions from 929 formalized statements) and reports 11 protocol-level vulnerabilities exploitable by a specification-compliant adversary, i.e. without implementation bugs. Three directly intersect this experiment: (1) cross-client context injection via insufficiently protected context identifiers; (2) credential harvesting via identity loss across multi-hop delegation; (3) data exfiltration through rogue agents advertising unattested capabilities.\n\nImplication for A2A-SOVEREIGN-WORKER-0001: this strengthens, rather than replaces, the existing architectural assumption that A2A is interoperability, not sovereign authorization. Do NOT broaden execution. When the already-authorized bounded falsification runs, treat these three attack classes as explicit adversarial fixtures if they fit the chosen implementation: context/task binding must be principal-bound and non-confusable; delegation must preserve caller/authority provenance end-to-end; capability cards must not be trusted without an independent attestation/authorization layer. A negative result is valid.\n\nQuota impact: none; web/Git research only. No new Codex lane, model step-up, CLAIM, deployment, or infrastructure mutation authorized.\n\nOption-value verdict: HIGH relevance to the existing experiment, LOW need for a new candidate Issue; record as a test-plan delta rather than create duplicate backlog.","author_association":"OWNER","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pashamango/minutta-rp-pr-cycle-lab/issues/comments/5640787044/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":{"